# .draugr.conf - GENERATED by `dr-forge gateway`. Re-running it reports this file as
# "differs - kept" once you have edited it, and -y overwrites it.
#
# The gateway runs no agent. It holds one allow-all proxy whose egress is filtered
# by the sbx policy. Read README.md before adding anything that filters.
DRAUGR_AGENT=shell
DRAUGR_SANDBOX=@SANDBOX@
DRAUGR_MEMORY=2g                          # default is 50% of host RAM, capped at 32 GiB
DRAUGR_CPUS=2
# 18888 is the proxy. 18445 is SMB, reached the same way and for the same reason:
# the host tunnel carries it into the guest, which mounts the vault through it.
# The guest dials 445, the only port its SMB client will use, so the tunnel maps
# guest 445 to this one.
#
# PUBLISHING 18888 IS WHAT MAKES THIS MOUND THE GATEWAY. dr-forge reads this key to
# decide whether the gateway is this repository, which it has to answer while
# everything is down - so it is a declared fact here rather than something probed.
#
# AND 18445 IS HERE ONLY IF THIS GATEWAY SERVES A VAULT. A published port is bound on
# the host by the publisher, not by whatever is meant to be behind it, so publishing
# it on a gateway with no share makes the host answer for a service nobody is running -
# and `dr-layer provision`'s vault phase reads exactly that listener to decide whether
# to install a share credential and REBOOT the guest for it. A port that lies costs a
# reboot. docs/FORGE.md, "2b's own five decisions".
DRAUGR_PORTS="@PORTS@"
DRAUGR_KIT=".draugr/kit kits/unreal kits/base"   # repo-relative, all versioned here
DRAUGR_MEM_SYNC=off
# Nothing is tracked by git here, so there is nothing to commit, review or push:
# the gateway is generated from Draugr's template and regenerated when the work
# changes. Whatever Draugr command reaches this directory first creates the
# repository under this key. docs/FORGE.md, "The gateway, generated".
DRAUGR_ON_MISSING_REPO=create-data-only
# The machine-wide config may mount things into every mound. The gateway runs no
# agent, so it gets nothing it does not need - except the two directories it serves
# to the VM: the vault holder read-only, and one scratch directory the VM may write.
# A mound reads every byte of what it mounts, so these are named exactly, never a
# whole drive.
DRAUGR_MOUNTS="@MOUNTS@"
DRAUGR_ENV=
# NOTHING TRAVELS. A gateway holds a proxy and an allowlist, and the mound already mounts
# this directory read-only - so there is no data to copy in or out, and dr-up must not try.
# Left empty deliberately rather than omitted: a reader should not have to know what an
# absent key defaults to, and in data-only mode an absent one used to mean "everything".
DRAUGR_DATA=
# The VM this gateway carries, so dr-forge works when run from here too.
DRAUGR_FORGE=@VM@
# NEVER STOPPED FOR BEING IDLE, because "idle" cannot be measured here. docs/FORGE.md,
# "Two mounds, two policies": *the gateway must be up whenever the VM is* - and the thing
# using it is a Windows VM on the host, which is not a mound and so cannot hold a claim.
# dr-dep counts a claim only while the CLAIMANT's mound runs, so a forge driven host-side
# has no valid claimant at all, and the 10m default then reads an hours-long engine install
# as nobody wanting the gateway.
#
# MEASURED, and it cost an afternoon: an engine download lost its proxy 16
# minutes in, to `dr-dep: nobody has used <gateway> for 10m; stopping it`. The guest's
# ssh tunnel kept forwarding 3128 into a host port that had stopped listening, so the only
# symptom was the Launcher reporting connection trouble.
#
# `dr-stop` is how a gateway goes down, and that is the right shape: it outlives the agent
# mounds that come and go around it. Set a duration here if you would rather it expired.
DRAUGR_LINGER=off
